              {
                "text": "**Analyzing the Input**\n\nI'm currently focused on dissecting the input grid structure, specifically the first row. It contains $K$ colored cells followed by zeros. I'm noting the dimensions: a 15x15 input grid with three cells of value 5 in the top row. The output is a 1x15 row, which is important to remember. I'm trying to see how to predict that.\n\n\n**Discovering Grid Patterns**\n\nI've made a breakthrough! I noticed a repeating pattern in the run lengths of the non-zero values: 1, 2, 3, 2, 1. I'm testing this against examples of different inputs. This sequence seems to wrap onto the grid width, and my hypothesis is now that this sequence determines the output.\n\n\n**Calculating Row Structure**\n\nI've realized the sequence run lengths follow a distinct structure, which is vital. Calculating the necessary rows using K (the maximum run length) appears key: ceil( (K^2 + 2K - 2) / width). I confirmed this with the Train 4 example. I'm excited to check if the same principle applies to other test inputs!\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
